 <h2 id="list"><code>list</code></h2>
-<p class="mod-desc">Representation decision — QWORD CELLS (the pankti/kosh convention), not byte strings. Reasoning: - A list is the growable analogue of pankti: same element model (one qword per element), same indexing, same literal passing (list_push(l, 42)). A byte-string list would be a string-list only, and would force every element through copy + length bookkeeping. Users with byte strings can store POINTERS (list_push(l, ptr)) — a qword list of pointers is the escape hatch, and it is the caller&#x27;s job to keep the pointed-to buffers alive. - No mixing: every element in this module is a qword. Do not pass byte-string buffers here expecting string semantics, and do not pass fmt/strb outputs as elements. - dasham elements are NOT supported. An untyped parameter truncates a dasham argument to its integer part (the R15 trap), so list_push(l, 3.7) stores 3. There is no way to store a dasham bit pattern through this API. Format floats first (fmt) if you need them as text, or keep a parallel structure. Two handle layouts (do NOT mix families on one handle): - list / stack handle: header [len, cap, data_ptr]; data is a separate cell array. The HANDLE address is stable across growth — only the data pointer inside it moves. - queue handle: header [len, cap, data_ptr, head]; a RING BUFFER (see queue_enqueue). queue_* on a list handle (or list_* on a queue handle) reads the wrong header and corrupts memory. Growth rule: when len == cap, capacity DOUBLES (cap *= 2), a new data array is allocated, elements are copied in logical order, and the header&#x27;s data pointer is updated. The old data array is NOT freed — the language has no deallocation primitive, so growth leaks the previous array (same as every other growing structure in the stdlib). Capacity never shrinks: list_clear and queue dequeue keep the allocated capacity. Allocation failure: if nirmmita returns 0 during growth, the operation returns 0 (or ok[0] = 0) and the structure is UNCHANGED. list_init returns 0 if either allocation fails. Out-of-bounds reads: list_get returns 0 for i outside [0, len). 0 is a legal stored value, so check list_len first when it matters. list_set / list_insert_at / list_remove_at return 1 ok / 0 out of bounds (or allocation failure for insert). stack_pop / stack_peek / queue_dequeue / queue_peek return the value and set ok[0] (a 1-cell pankti) to 1 on success, 0 when empty — the value alone cannot signal emptiness. All signatures stay within the 6-argument cap.</p>
