 <h2 id="map"><code>map</code></h2>
-<p class="mod-desc">Open addressing with linear probing over parallel pankti arrays. KEY REPRESENTATION: byte strings (the R10 strb_* surface), NOT char-code arrays. Rationale: keys are usually literals (&quot;apple&quot;), and byte strings let the caller pass a literal directly — no hand-built cell array. The hash (lib/hash.smlib fnv1a, via `ayojan hash` below) is byte-based (char_at), so the choice is consistent end to end. Worked example: ayojan map mukhya() { vitti m = map_init(8) map_put(m, 8, &quot;apple&quot;, 5, 42) likha(map_get(m, 8, &quot;apple&quot;, 5))   # 42 likha(&quot;\n&quot;) } This matches lib/string.smlib&#x27;s header: a byte string is bytes packed 1-per-byte (a literal, or a set_char-built buffer), measured with an explicit length, read with char_at. Do not pass char-code arrays here. Layout of the map buffer m (qword cells, except the key bytes): m[0]           capacity m[1]           live entry count m[2+i]         state[i]   (0 = empty, 1 = live), i in 0..cap-1 m[2+cap+i]     keylen[i] m[2+2*cap+i]   val[i] key bytes: byte region at byte offset (2+3*cap)*8, KEY_MAX bytes/slot. KEY_MAX = 32: keys longer than 32 bytes are truncated to 32, consistently on put/get/contains/remove (documented; excluded from the properties). cap must be &gt;= 1 (clamped). Probing wraps modulo cap. DELETION is backward-shift (Knuth 6.4R): the freed slot is filled by shifting later probe-chain entries back toward their home bucket, so there is no &quot;deleted&quot; state and no tombstones — a lookup stops at the first empty slot and never misses a live entry. ABSENT SENTINEL: map_get returns -1 (0-1) for a missing key. Values are programmer-managed integers; do not store -1 as a value unless you disambiguate with map_contains first. FULL TABLE: map_put returns 0 and changes nothing.</p>
